# Project standards

## Scope and design
- Implement the supplied Portfolio/Stock exercise in small, explainable steps.
- Keep data in memory and use supplied prices; return a trade proposal without executing orders.
- Keep persistence, HTTP endpoints, a frontend, and deployment as optional extensions.
- Keep portfolio calculations in ordinary Python classes without Django imports.
- Keep commands and optional views thin; delegate business behavior to feature classes or services.
- Organize by feature (`portfolio`); extract services when they have a concrete responsibility.
- Add a module or abstraction when it has a concrete responsibility; keep related code together.

## Python and numeric correctness
- Use English identifiers, Python naming conventions, and type hints on public functions.
- Use Decimal for prices, quantities, and weights; construct it from strings or integers, not floats.
- Agree and document numeric precision, rounding, and financial assumptions before relying on them.
- Validate inputs with useful errors; do not silently repair invalid data or swallow exceptions.
- Keep calculations deterministic for the same inputs and leave the supplied portfolio unchanged.
- Write short comments or docstrings for intent, assumptions, or non-obvious behavior.
- Use Ruff for linting and formatting; type hints need a separate checker for static enforcement.

## Verification and CI
- Test observable behavior and domain invariants with standard-library unittest.
- Cover balanced and unbalanced portfolios, invalid inputs, and agreed boundary cases.
- Keep core tests independent of network access, external services, and a database.
- Run checks relevant to each change; report their results and material limitations.
- Add GitHub Actions after meaningful tests exist: install pinned dependencies, lint, check formatting, run tests.
- Keep local and CI checks equivalent; deployment is not required for the challenge.

## Environment and usability
- Use an isolated environment and pinned dependencies; choose supported releases deliberately.
- Keep secrets, virtual environments, generated caches, and local data out of version control.
- Make the example reproducible and label trade direction, ticker, quantity, and monetary units clearly.
- Make invalid command input produce a clear message and a nonzero exit status.
- If a frontend is added, support narrow and wide screens, keyboard use, labels, and useful validation feedback.
- Reuse frontend components for repeated UI or behavior; avoid copying business rules across screens.
- Keep calculation rules in Python; an optional frontend should consume their results.

## Documentation and collaboration
- Keep the README focused on setup, execution, an example, assumptions, checks, and limitations.
- Distinguish exercise requirements, agreed assumptions, and pending decisions.
- Explain meaningful decisions before implementing them; avoid generating the entire solution at once.
- Include the actual LLM conversation record before submission; do not present a summary as a full transcript.
